INT. NATATORIUM - POOL DECK - 5:30 A.M.

Chlorine fog. Blue water, glass-flat. A scoreboard reading 00:00.00. On the bench: three swimmers wrapped in towels. COACH RENATA BOSCH, forties, with a whistle and a stopwatch, paces the deck.

MARCUS TULLY, twenties, anchor leg, stares at the water. DOLORES AMADI, twenties, lead-off, blows into her hands. A NEW KID, sixteen, in a team parka too big for him, sits at the very end of the bench with his goggles in his lap.

RENATA
(to the group)
Warm-up in five. Four hundred easy, then pace sets.

MARCUS
(to Dolores, low)
You hear about the sponsor?

DOLORES
Pulled out.

NEW KID
(to himself)
Don't ask. Don't ask.

RENATA
Kid. You are?

NEW KID
Sunny.

RENATA
Sunny what?

SUNNY
Sunny Park.

RENATA
Sunny, you swim third leg.

MARCUS
Third? That's my relay split.

DOLORES
That's what I said.

RENATA
I'm not asking.

MARCUS
Coach, he's sixteen.

SUNNY
Seventeen in March.

DOLORES
Marcus, he's fast.

MARCUS
I didn't say he wasn't.

RENATA
(blowing the whistle)
Everybody in!

They all drop their towels and dive. Sunny hesitates. Renata stops beside him.

RENATA (CONT'D)
Sunny. It's only water.

SUNNY
(muttering)
That's what scares me.
